 ### Edge Cases and Pitfalls
-*   **Empty Chunks**: If no voxels are visible or pass projection, the pipeline returns early [S2, unofficial].
-*   **Precision**: Floating-point precision in world space transformation and distance calculations may affect sorting accuracy [S2, unofficial].
-*   **Boundary Conditions**: The bounding box calculation must correctly handle chunks at the edge of the voxel grid [S1, unofficial].
+*   **GPU Overhead:** Instancing entire cubes individually causes huge GPU overhead relative to the small triangle count (12 triangles per cube). This overhead worsens as GPU speed increases [S1, unofficial]. Baking into chunks eliminates this [S1, unofficial].
+*   **Small Batches:** Small batches are inefficient [S1, unofficial].
+*   **Visibility vs. Rendering:** Do not use an octree structure for the rendering loop itself. Use it exclusively for processing visibility (determining which chunks to add/remove from the active list) or collisions [S1, unofficial].
+*   **VR Frustum:** For VR, create a frustum box that encompasses both eyes to perform a single visibility check instead of two [S1, unofficial].
 
 ## Operating characteristics
-*   **Time Complexity**: Dominated by geometry generation ($O(N)$ where $N$ is the number of voxels in the chunk) and sorting ($O(M \log M)$ where $M$ is the number of visible faces).
-*   **Memory Complexity**: Proportional to the number of visible faces in the chunk.
-*   **Performance**: High throughput due to batching and culling [S2, unofficial].
-*   **Strengths**: Efficient culling, support for visual effects, and depth sorting for transparency [S2, unofficial].
-*   **Limitations**: Sorting overhead for large numbers of faces; potential z-fighting if not handled [S2, unofficial].
-*   **When to Use**: Scenes with large volumes of transparent or semi-transparent voxels requiring correct depth ordering [S2, unofficial].
-*   **When Not to Use**: Real-time applications with extremely high voxel counts where sorting becomes a bottleneck; opaque scenes might benefit from other methods like ray marching or ray tracing.
+*   **Time Complexity:** Chunk generation time depends on processor speed and content density. On a quad-core i7 2.2GHz with GTX 560M, the system generates >10 chunks per second [S1, unofficial]. On a BeagleBoard (single-core ARM Cortex-A8 at 720MHz), it manages about 10 chunks per second [S1, unofficial].
+*   **Memory Complexity:** Memory usage is proportional to the number of active chunks and the chunk size ($16 \times 16 \times 16$ voxels per chunk) [S1, unofficial].
+*   **Strengths:** Eliminates hidden faces, reduces draw calls, separates geometry generation from rendering (allowing asynchronous loading), and mitigates GPU overhead associated with small batches [S1, unofficial].
+*   **Limitations:** Requires pre-baking or asynchronous generation of chunk geometry, which adds complexity to the loading pipeline. The render loop relies on a simple list traversal, which may not be optimal for extremely sparse or highly dynamic scenes without spatial indexing [S1, unofficial].
+*   **When to Use:** High-throughput voxel rendering where fill rate and generation bottlenecks are critical, and the scene has static or semi-static geometry that can be batched into chunks [S1, unofficial].
+*   **When Not to Use:** Scenes requiring frequent, fine-grained dynamic updates to individual voxels, or where memory bandwidth is severely constrained by large VBO uploads.
 
 ## Background
-The Chunk-based Voxel Rendering Pipeline is implemented in the `3D-Mesh-Voxelizer` project [S1, unofficial] and the `pyvorengi-sdk-demo` [S2, unofficial]. It represents a practical approach to rendering voxel data by leveraging standard graphics pipeline stages (transform, clip, sort, rasterize) [S2, unofficial].
+The method was discussed in the context of overcoming fill rate and generation bottlenecks in voxel engines [S1, unofficial]. It was proposed as an alternative to naive voxel rendering (instancing individual cubes) to improve performance on both high-end GPUs and low-power embedded devices like the BeagleBoard [S1, unofficial]. The approach separates the spatial data structure (octree) used for visibility culling from the rendering pipeline, which uses a simple list of active chunks [S1, unofficial].
 
